<SiteLayout title="Module 9: Address Resolution — fred.fish" description="Source-based lessons on ARP, IPv6 Neighbor Discovery, and local link-layer address resolution." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 09" label="MODULE 9 / ADDRESS RESOLUTION" /><h1>Find the next-hop address.<br /><span>Deliver the frame.</span></h1><p>IP addresses identify network-layer destinations, but local Ethernet delivery needs a MAC address. Address-resolution protocols connect those two layers.</p></header>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Why address resolution is necessary</h2><p>When a host needs to send an IP packet over an Ethernet LAN, it needs the MAC address of the next device on the local link. For a local destination, that is the destination host. For a remote destination, it is the default gateway. IPv4 uses Address Resolution Protocol (ARP); IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery, which is carried in ICMPv6 messages.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0651 · IMG-0746</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Follow an IPv4 ARP exchange</h2><p>A host checks its ARP cache for a mapping between the target IPv4 address and a MAC address. If no valid entry exists, it sends an ARP Request as a Layer 2 broadcast. The device that owns the target IPv4 address sends an ARP Reply, normally as a unicast frame. The sender caches the mapping for a period of time and uses it to build the Ethernet frame.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>ARP requests · ARP replies · ARP table</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Understand IPv6 Neighbor Discovery</h2><p>IPv6 does not use ARP. Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP) uses ICMPv6 messages. A Neighbor Solicitation (NS) asks for information about a neighbor, and a Neighbor Advertisement (NA) provides the response. Neighbor Discovery also supports functions such as Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) and router discovery.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0665 · IMG-0674 · IMG-1002</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Diagnose address-resolution problems</h2><p>If a host cannot reach a local neighbor, inspect its IPv4 address and subnet mask or IPv6 address and prefix, check the local link, and examine the ARP or neighbor cache. A stale or incorrect mapping can prevent frame delivery even when the IP configuration appears correct. ARP spoofing abuses the lack of authentication in ordinary ARP; switches and network security controls can reduce exposure.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPICS</span><code>ARP cache · Neighbor cache · Network security</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Resolve an IP address to a local link address.</h2><p>Practice ARP broadcasts, neighbor messages, and the role of the default gateway.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
